        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "Google\u0027s Antigravity Teamwork framework, paired with Gemini 3.7 Flash, enabled teams of autonomous AI agents to solve seven previously open academic math problems—including Knuth\u0027s Cycles Conjecture—and build a cycle-accurate RISC-V CPU simulator from scratch that boots the xv6 operating system. The system achieved 71% accuracy on TCSBench, a rigorous theoretical computer science benchmark. The agents also contributed performance improvements to established open-source libraries including Eigen and ParlayHash.",
        "execSummary":  "Google\u0027s Antigravity results represent the most credible public demonstration to date of multi-agent AI systems solving genuine expert-level problems that human teams had not resolved—a threshold moment for enterprise leaders evaluating agentic AI\u0027s ceiling. The math and chip-design results are narrow but prove the orchestration pattern works at research-grade difficulty, which has direct implications for complex enterprise workflows like software architecture, financial modeling, and supply chain optimization. For leaders investing in agent orchestration platforms, this signals that the bottleneck is shifting from model capability to the orchestration layer itself—how agents divide work, verify each other\u0027s outputs, and coordinate on shared state. The enterprise caveat: these benchmarks ran in controlled research environments; production deployment of comparable orchestration at enterprise scale remains an unsolved engineering problem.",
        "vendorLean":  "Google — promotes Google\u0027s Antigravity framework and Gemini models",
